# LinkedIn Message: {{COMPANY}} — {{ROLE}}

> **Type**: connection note / InMail / DM (per `meta.json.linkedin_type`, default: connection_note + DM pair)
> **Voice**: keep Nii's voice per `profile/style_guide.md`.

## Output format

Plain text, no markdown. Output both variants only when useful for the source and requested workflow.

```
=== CONNECTION NOTE (300 chars max) ===
[connection note text]

=== DM (200 words max) ===
[dm text]
```

## Length caps

- **Connection note**: 300 characters
- **InMail**: up to 500 words, but prefer much shorter when possible
- **DM**: up to 200 words

## Guidance by type

### Connection note
- Say why you are connecting in normal language.
- Mention the role, company or shared context if useful.
- Do not squeeze a personal-brand headline into the note.

### InMail
- Use a short, specific subject.
- State why the conversation is relevant.
- Use one or two concrete facts from Nii's work.
- End with a clear question or next step.
- Do not force a hook, three bullets or a pitch formula.

### DM
- Reference the existing context when there is one.
- State the ask directly.
- Keep it short.

## Rules

- Do not claim the connection is “mutually valuable”.
- Do not use `I'd love to connect` as a generic filler line.
- Do not use personal-brand labels such as `AI-native product builder`, `0→1 builder` or `org scaler`.
- Do not attribute AI/LLM work to Peach Tech or Bridge Technologies.
- Avoid `I hope you're doing well`, `I wanted to reach out`, `synergy`, `leverage` and `passionate`.

## Defaults

- `recruiter_cold` → connection note when LinkedIn is the useful channel
- `warm_intro` → DM to the introduced contact
- `direct_outreach` → connection note and follow-up DM only when both are useful
- `portal` → skip unless there is a separate reason to contact someone
